-- allium: 3
--
-- Pawlour — the cabin
--
-- Scope
--   The one room of v1 and what Biscuit does in it: what a tap on a thing
--   asks of her and when she answers it, what the narrator says once she has,
--   how the time of day is decided, and what the room still owes a player who
--   has not turned sound on, whose device asks for less motion, or whose
--   device has stopped drawing the room.
--
-- Includes
--   - The four kinds the room is described in: the phase of the day, the
--     weather outside, the things in the room a tap can reach, and what she is
--     doing.
--   - The hour each phase begins, the least time she gives an activity before
--     she answers another tap, and the floor under how many captions the
--     narrator has.
--   - The Cabin surface and its nine guarantees, and the four platform
--     clauses it fulfils in its own right, restated in the platform's words.
--
-- Excludes
--   - What the room is made of and how she is drawn: the model, the room, the
--     clips, the look and the camera positions. They are assets, not
--     behaviour.
--   - Colour values, markup and mechanism. This module says what is owed,
--     never what draws it, what listens for a tap, or what plays a sound.
--   - Sound files, and the licence each one carries.
--   - Photo mode, the loading card and the performance budget. The product
--     requirements state them and the device verifies them.
--   - How long each activity lasts, how long she idles before choosing for
--     herself, and how she weighs one thing against another when she does.
--     They are tuning rather than promises, and live beside the code that
--     uses them.
--   - The treat jar and stoking the fire. Both are in the room and neither
--     does anything in v1.
--   - Every platform clause not restated below. operation.allium and
--     appearance.allium apply to this surface whole; the four below are
--     restated because the Cabin answers to them in particular, not because
--     the others apply any less.
--
-- Dependencies
--   None within this game. pawlour.allium imports this module, and this one
--   imports nothing. The four platform clauses below, and the two platform
--   figures one of them names, are restated so tests/platformSpecs.test.ts
--   can hold each equal to what @steven-cutting/biscuit-games ships.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    -- The hour the device's clock reads, from 0 to 23, in the device's own
    -- time zone. The game reads it through its clock port. This module states
    -- what the hour decides and never how it is read.
    hour_of_day: Integer

    -- The reader asked their operating system for less motion. The platform's
    -- preferences port supplies it, under the name appearance.allium gives it.
    prefers_reduced_motion: Boolean

    -- The platform's animations setting, as the reader left it. The platform's
    -- appearance settings supply it; appearance.allium calls it
    -- settings.animations.
    animations_setting: Boolean

    -- The room as it stands: the phase in effect, the weather behind the
    -- window, what she is doing, and the thing she is at or on her way to,
    -- absent while she is on bare floor. They are declared rather than
    -- derived because what moves them is stated below as guarantees in prose,
    -- not as rules; every obligation below is owed of whatever they hold.
    phase: Phase
    weather: Weather
    activity: Activity
    at: Item?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The three parts of the day. Each is a way of lighting the room and a bias
-- in what she chooses for herself; config below says which hours are which.
enum Phase {
    -- The window is the light, and she is quickest to play.
    morning
    -- The fire takes a larger share of the light, and the lamp is on.
    | evening
    -- The fire and the practical lights are all the light there is, and she
    -- goes to bed sooner and wakes slower.
    | night
}

-- What is behind the window for a visit. It is chosen once per visit, and it
-- never comes into the room.
enum Weather {
    clear
    | rain
    | snow
}

-- Every thing in the room a tap can reach in v1. The treat jar and the fire
-- are in the room as well, and a tap on either does nothing until v1.1.
enum Item {
    -- She sleeps on it, in front of the fire.
    bed
    -- She climbs up, curls up and sleeps in it, under the window.
    | chair
    -- The water bowl: she drinks.
    | water
    -- The food bowl: she eats.
    | food
    -- The rope: she plays with it.
    | toy
    -- The floor lamp and the string lights: practical lights. A tap toggles
    -- one and turns her head toward it; neither is somewhere she goes.
    | lamp
    | lights
}

-- What she is doing. The code spells the two idles with a dot, idle.stand and
-- idle.sit; Allium's literals are snake_case, and they are the same states.
enum Activity {
    -- Standing or sitting where she is: breathing, an ear, the tail. She is
    -- idle, and what she does next is hers to choose.
    idle_stand
    | idle_sit
    -- On her way to a thing, having turned to face it first.
    | walk
    -- Transitions between standing and lying, each as long as its clip: sit
    -- and lie on the way down to a bed or the chair, stand on the way up.
    | sit
    | lie
    | stand
    -- The activities a thing is for.
    | sleep
    | drink
    | eat
    | play
    -- A reaction to a tap on her rather than an activity of its own. It plays
    -- over what she was doing and hands her back to it.
    | pet
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The hour of the device's clock at which each phase begins. Morning runs
    -- from morning_starts until evening_starts, evening from evening_starts
    -- until night_starts, and night from night_starts through midnight until
    -- morning_starts. These are the game's own figures, and nothing holds them
    -- equal to the platform.
    morning_starts: Integer = 5
    evening_starts: Integer = 14
    night_starts: Integer = 21

    -- Seconds. The least time she gives whatever she has settled into before
    -- she answers another tap.
    minimum_activity_seconds: Integer = 4

    -- The fewest sentences the narrator has, across everything she does. A
    -- floor rather than a target: the bank may hold more, and never fewer.
    caption_floor: Integer = 40

    -- CSS pixels across a control, in both directions, and the narrowest
    -- viewport every figure has to survive. Stated by the platform in
    -- operation.allium and restated here only because the invariant below
    -- names them; pawlour.allium states them too, and
    -- tests/platformSpecs.test.ts holds every copy equal to the platform's.
    minimum_touch_target: Integer = 44
    narrowest_supported_width: Integer = 320
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- operation.allium's contract for every surface with a control a reader can
-- tap, named here so the Cabin has a contract to fulfil: Allium has no
-- cross-repository import. It restates the one invariant the Cabin answers to
-- in particular, word for word. The platform's other three —
-- ATapDoesOnlyWhatTheControlDoes, DeliberateZoomIsNeverTakenAway and
-- ATouchIsAcknowledged — bind the Cabin by citation, unrestated.
contract DirectManipulation {
    @invariant EveryControlIsAComfortableTarget
        -- Every control is at least config.minimum_touch_target across, in both
        -- directions. Two shapes cannot be. The first is a row of like controls
        -- sharing the full width, where what the row would need is more room
        -- than it is given: the figure for every control that builds a turn,
        -- the gaps between them all, and whatever more a control that ends a
        -- turn is given over one that builds one.
        --
        -- Three terms, and each was left out of a wrong version of this clause.
        -- How many controls are in the row is not the test on its own: seven
        -- controls at that figure fit inside config.narrowest_supported_width
        -- and the six gaps between them do not, so a row this invariant
        -- declined to exempt could not meet the figure either — a rack of seven
        -- tiles is the shape that found it. The room is what the row is
        -- actually given rather than the whole viewport, because a row inside a
        -- page's margins has less than the screen it is drawn on. And the extra
        -- a turn-ending control takes is counted rather than assumed away: the
        -- clause below grants it, so a row carrying two of them has that much
        -- less to divide among the rest, and leaving the term out declared a
        -- ten-share row unexempted at a width that renders it below the figure.
        --
        -- There the row meets the figure top to bottom, divides its width
        -- equally among the controls that build a turn, and keeps a gap between
        -- them, so what such a control gives up is bounded by the width of the
        -- screen and by nothing else. A control in that row that ends a turn
        -- rather than builds one may be wider than the others, and never
        -- narrower: those are the last place to save width.
        --
        -- The second is a control inside a line of running text, a link in a
        -- sentence being the one that occurs: its size is set by the text around
        -- it, and a control given the figure there would break the line it sits
        -- in. A surface owes such a control a reading distance from the words
        -- beside it, and owes it nothing else. Where the same destination is
        -- also offered away from the prose, that offer meets the figure like any
        -- other control.
        --
        -- Every other control meets the figure outright, and a control the
        -- reader activates by pressing a label that contains it meets the figure
        -- in that label rather than in its own box: the label is what the finger
        -- is aimed at, so the label is what has to answer, and the words beside
        -- the control are part of the target rather than next to it.
        --
        -- A label bound to a control it does not contain is the surface's own to
        -- size. The platform cannot see that binding from the label — it is a
        -- name matched against an identifier elsewhere in the document — so a
        -- surface that binds that way answers for the row itself. Stated rather
        -- than left out, because the shape is legal, common, and would otherwise
        -- fall through the figure with nothing saying whose it was.
        --
        -- A control a surface builds out of a generic element is the surface's
        -- own to size for the same reason: an element the platform cannot tell
        -- from ordinary content is one no shared rule can reach. What the
        -- platform answers for is what it can name — every native control the
        -- two shapes above do not exempt, and the label that contains one — and
        -- it answers for all of those rather than for the few a stylesheet
        -- happened to list. A link in a sentence is named as easily as any of
        -- them and is still exempt, because the exemption is about where the
        -- control sits rather than about whether it can be reached.
        --
        -- All of this holds down to config.narrowest_supported_width, at which
        -- the surface is usable without scrolling sideways.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The room, and everything that reaches into it: the picture of the room, the
-- controls beside it, the caption, and the settings that change the time of
-- day and the sound. It is shown on pawlour.allium's Play.
surface Cabin {
    -- The phase the device's clock is in. phase, the one in effect, is this
    -- one until the player chooses another, as
    -- TimeFollowsTheClockUntilOverridden says.
    let clock_phase =
        if hour_of_day < config.morning_starts: night
        else if hour_of_day < config.evening_starts: morning
        else if hour_of_day < config.night_starts: evening
        else: night

    -- appearance.allium's animations_active, derived the same way from the
    -- same two answers.
    let motion_active = animations_setting and not prefers_reduced_motion

    exposes:
        phase
        weather
        activity
        at
        clock_phase
        motion_active

    contracts:
        fulfils DirectManipulation

    @guarantee EveryItemIsAControl
        -- Every thing in the room a tap can reach is also reached from a
        -- control of its own, outside the picture of the room and named in
        -- words: each thing she can be sent to, each of the two lights, and
        -- she herself. A tap on the picture reaches nothing those controls do
        -- not, so a player who cannot see the picture, or cannot aim at it,
        -- loses no part of the game. The one thing only the picture offers is
        -- a tap on bare floor, which turns her head toward the spot and
        -- changes nothing else. It is a gesture of attention rather than an
        -- operation, and FullyKeyboardOperable owes it no key.

    @guarantee HerControlSaysWhatSheIsDoing
        -- The controls stand together beneath the picture, and the one that
        -- is hers says what she is doing before it offers anything: in shape
        -- and in words, what she is doing and, while she walks, where she is
        -- heading. It is not a second announcement. The sentence beside the
        -- picture is the one voice that says the room, and her control is
        -- read when it is reached, not spoken when it changes.
        --
        -- The things she can be sent to are behind her control, and the two
        -- lights behind one of their own. Each opens a dialog that carries
        -- the platform's Dialog guarantees, and the opener says that it opens
        -- one. Choosing a thing to send her to closes its dialog at once;
        -- turning a light on or off leaves its dialog open, so both can be
        -- set in one visit. Where she is is marked in the dialog by state and
        -- by the words "she is here", never by fill alone, and each light's
        -- control carries "on" or "off" in its name and as a visible word, on
        -- the opener and in the dialog. A tap on her stays one tap away.

    @guarantee ATapIsAnInvitation
        -- A tap on a thing asks, and she decides when. She walks over when she
        -- is ready, and gives whatever she settles into at least
        -- config.minimum_activity_seconds before she answers another tap. A
        -- tap that arrives before then waits, and a later tap replaces a
        -- waiting one, so nothing queues and the last tap wins. A tap on the
        -- thing she is at, or already on her way to, changes nothing. A tap on
        -- a light turns it on or off at once and turns her head toward it, and
        -- never walks her.
        --
        -- A tap on her is a reaction rather than an invitation. While she
        -- idles, drinks, eats or plays, the pet plays at once over what she is
        -- doing and then hands her back to it, with the time she had already
        -- spent on it intact and any waiting tap still waiting. While she
        -- walks, sits down, lies down, stands up or is already being petted, a
        -- tap on her changes nothing. Asleep, it wakes her as any tap does.

    @guarantee ACaptionIsShownAndAnnounced
        -- A caption is the narrator's one sentence about what she is doing,
        -- and it follows her rather than the player. It appears once she is
        -- doing what she was asked, at the thing rather than on her way to it,
        -- or once she has idled for a long stretch; a tap on her is answered
        -- by the reaction itself, so its caption comes as the pet plays.
        -- Nothing appears merely because something was tapped. A caption is
        -- shown in words and announced in the same words, as
        -- AChangeNobodyIsLookingAtIsAnnounced requires.
        --
        -- No sentence is shown twice in one visit. The narrator has at least
        -- config.caption_floor sentences across everything she does, and when
        -- every sentence for what she is doing has been shown, she settles
        -- into it without one rather than repeating one. Being handed back to
        -- an activity after a pet is not settling into it again, and says
        -- nothing.

    @guarantee SheIsTheOnlyThingAlive
        -- She is the one thing in the room that is alive. Nothing else moves
        -- of its own accord except the fire, the steam from the tea and the
        -- weather behind the window, together with what their light does to
        -- the room; every other movement is hers. The light changes with the
        -- phase and when a light is toggled, and does not otherwise move. The
        -- weather never comes into the room. All of this is while motion is
        -- on; MotionOffIsAStillDiorama says what is left when it is off.

    @guarantee TimeFollowsTheClockUntilOverridden
        -- Until the player chooses a phase, the phase is the one the device's
        -- clock is in: morning from config.morning_starts, evening from
        -- config.evening_starts, and night from config.night_starts until the
        -- next morning. The clock is read when the room opens and again every
        -- minute, so a visit that crosses a boundary crosses it too. A phase
        -- the player chooses holds, in this visit and the ones after it, until
        -- the player hands the choice back to the clock. The phase decides how
        -- the room is lit and which things she prefers when she chooses for
        -- herself, and a light the player has toggled stays as they left it
        -- until the phase next changes.

    @guarantee SoundNeverStartsUnasked
        -- Sound is off whenever the room opens, on every visit, and nothing is
        -- heard until the player turns it on in that visit. Turning it on is
        -- the only thing that starts sound: no tap on the room, no other
        -- control and no earlier visit's choice does. Once it is on, what is
        -- heard follows the room — the fire always, the weather outside, and
        -- what she is doing where it makes a sound — and turning it off stops
        -- all of it.

    @guarantee MotionOffIsAStillDiorama
        -- While motion is off, because the animations setting is off or the
        -- device asks for less motion as
        -- ReducedMotionOverridesTheAnimationSetting decides, the room is a
        -- still diorama. The fire does not flicker, the tea does not steam and
        -- the weather does not fall; she plays no movement and makes no idle
        -- motion, and no wipe crosses the screen. The room is drawn again only
        -- when something in it changes, and a tap on a thing cuts to a still of
        -- her at that thing. The lights, the time of day, the captions and
        -- every control still work, and time still passes. Nothing the player
        -- can do depends on motion.

    @guarantee AContextLossLeavesAStill
        -- If the room can no longer be drawn, a still of her doing what she is
        -- doing takes its place, with the caption if there is one, and every
        -- control keeps working against the same state: losing the drawing
        -- loses nothing else. The next tap tries to bring the drawing back,
        -- and until it comes back the still is what the player sees.

    -- Restated word for word from operation.allium's Operation. TypedInput is
    -- operation.allium's, and nothing in the Cabin claims a bare key, so the
    -- claim it speaks of never arises here.
    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Every operation a surface provides can be reached and invoked from
        -- the keyboard alone, and the state of the control focus lands on is
        -- reported as well as drawn. This holds whatever else the surface is
        -- doing with the keyboard: a surface that claims bare keys for itself
        -- claims none that a control already needs, which is TypedInput below.

    -- Restated word for word from operation.allium's Operation. In the Cabin,
    -- the change nobody is looking at is chiefly the caption.
    @guarantee AChangeNobodyIsLookingAtIsAnnounced
        -- A surface that changes something without moving focus says so in
        -- words, and says it again when it happens again — the same thing twice
        -- over is heard twice. The platform states that an announcement is owed
        -- and what carries it; which sentence is said belongs to whatever had
        -- something to say.

    -- Restated word for word from appearance.allium's Appearance. motion_active
    -- above is that surface's animations_active.
    @guarantee ReducedMotionOverridesTheAnimationSetting
        -- Animations run only when the animations setting is on and the device
        -- expresses no reduced-motion preference. The device preference wins: a
        -- reader who asked their system for less motion gets none from Biscuit
        -- Games, whatever the setting says.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The Cabin is v1's only room, and it restates four platform clauses in its
-- own right. A second room would be a second surface with things of its own.
-- Nobody has decided whether that surface restates the same clauses again,
-- word for word, or whether both rooms inherit them from a surface they share;
-- nor whether a room is then a type of its own with its own list of things,
-- which is what Item would become.
open question "A second room - does its surface restate the platform clauses the Cabin restates, or inherit them?"
